course: dictionary
lesson: 3090
title: Spooky words and mood
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words do more than give a meaning. They set a mood, a feeling the reader gets from the story. Eerie is one of these words. Look at the context: «Red foxes live in many British towns and cities, and the eerie screams on winter nights are often foxes calling.» The screams are strange and a little frightening, and they are heard at night, so eerie fits.
explain: Eerie means strange and frightening in a quiet, mysterious way, so it can make you feel uneasy. Writers use it in ghost stories and spooky settings: empty houses, fog, silence and owls at night. Notice the clue words around it. Quiet, night and calling all add to the eerie feeling.
explain: Eerily is the adverb, and it tells you how something happens. «The house was eerily quiet.» Eerily tells you the quiet was strange in a spooky way. Try swapping in your meaning: "The house was strangely quiet, in a spooky way." It still makes sense, so you have found the right meaning.
fact: Eerie means strange and frightening in a quiet, mysterious way.
fact: Eerily means strangely, as in «eerily quiet».
fact: Eerie is an adjective, so it describes a noun: an eerie silence, an eerie glow, an eerie feeling.
fact: «The eerie screams on winter nights are often foxes calling.» Context shows the screams are spooky, but they are really foxes.
fact: Writers use eerie to build suspense, so the reader feels nervous and wants to read on.
exercise: «The eerie screams on winter nights are often foxes calling.» What does eerie mean? | *Strange and frightening in a quiet, mysterious way | Bright and colourful | Warm and sleepy | Loud and cheerful
exercise: «The house was eerily quiet.» What does eerily mean here? | *Strangely | Slowly | Happily | Very loudly
exercise: Why might a ghost story writer choose the word eerie? | *It builds a creepy, uneasy feeling for the reader | It means happy and bright | It means very tall | It is a longer word than spooky
test: «The forest had an eerie glow.» What is an eerie glow like? | A cheerful fire in the hearth | Loud thunder and rain | Bright, warm sunshine | *Strange, faint and spooky light
test: The wind was ___ tonight than last night. Choose the right word. | Eerily | *Eerier | Eeriest | Eerie
exam: Which word is an adverb meaning strangely? | eerie | spooky | *eerily | eerier
